+export const INTEG_INFO = `The <b>Integration</b> card integrates again the raw chromatograms of the thermal-conductivity detector (TCD), the files of a column per injection that the GC software writes beside its table, and hands the results to the <b>Production Rate</b> cards below it. Each injection's <b>H₂ and O₂</b> are fitted together in one <b>window</b> of retention time (268–326 s by default): a smooth baseline (a straight line, with the <b>step</b> the detector makes after O₂), a peak shape for H₂, for O₂ and for the system peak at 310 s (<b>S310</b>, fitted so that its tail does not end up in O₂'s area; the step and S310 are drawn but go no further). The fit is made in <b>two passes</b>: the first, with a robust loss, finds <b>spikes</b> and <b>glitch zones</b> (marked on the plot in red), which the second, an ordinary least-squares fit, leaves out, so that a spike is never read as gas; a fit that looks wrong is started again from a generic start, and the better of the two kept. The <b>method</b> is the card's, in the numbers under the chromatogram, which are its source of truth: the window's edges (the green lines on the chromatogram drag them as an aid, snapping to the data's 0.2 s, and write into the numbers) and each gas's trapezoid base intervals (numbers only: they are 10–100 times more sensitive than the window's edges, and O₂'s right one sits on the step). Moving an edge by 1–2 s changes the areas by a few hundredths of a per cent; by 5 s it reaches the peaks the model has no shape for, the system peak at 262–267.5 s and N₂ from about 330.5 s (grey on the chromatogram), and the fit degrades: the card says so. The area of each peak is its fitted shape's, ± its standard deviation; χ²<sub>r</sub> says how well the model follows the data (about 1: within the noise). Beside it, a <b>trapezoid</b> area is worked out the classic way (a straight base between two stretches either side of the peak); for H₂ the two should agree within a few per cent, and the card marks (a ring on the overview, ⚠ in the list) an injection where they differ by more than 3 % (and by more than 0.0015 %mol), where χ²<sub>r</sub> is above 4 after the fit's restart (some fits are fragile: the robust pass stops at its 100th evaluation), where a glitch was left out or where the fit did not converge. O₂'s trapezoids, whose right base sits on the step, read well under its fit at every injection: shown, not marked. The <b>run overview</b> shows, for the file chosen, every injection's fit (dot), trapezoids (square) and the software table's value (cross), against the injection time (the injection's number when there is no table); a click on a point shows its chromatogram. Integration runs by itself in the background when raw chromatograms are loaded, or a little after the method changes; <b>Stop</b> stops it, <b>Re-integrate</b> runs what is left. A changed window integrates every file again; a file added, only itself. <b>Pairs</b>: a table and its raw chromatograms (the same name with _TCD) share a row of the file list; the table's rows with a date are its injections, which must be as many as the chromatograms (else the card says so, and Analysis keeps the table). <b>Concentrations</b>: <b>Calibration…</b> opens the standards' window: drop their raw chromatograms, type each standard's H₂ and O₂ (blank: not a standard of that gas), choose the unit and, per gas, a free intercept (the default: O₂'s response has a real intercept, about −500 ppm, and a line forced through zero biases its low levels by −8 to −26 % while R² barely moves; each level's deviation from the line is shown, since R² cannot tell) or a zero one; the line area = slope × c + intercept is fitted to them and a sample's concentration is (area − intercept) / slope. Standards always win. Without them, the card <b>takes the calibration from the GC software's table</b>: for each pair, k is the median of our area over the table's value across the injections well above the noise (a table value of at least 0.02 %mol for H₂, 0.012 %mol for O₂, ten injections at least), and the concentration is area / k (an info alert says so). <b>The risks of a calibration taken from the GC software</b>, which standards avoid:<ul class="gci-risks"><li>it <b>borrows the software's scale</b>, and assumes that the software's areas are proportional to ours, which a different baseline or peak split breaks (for O₂, on H₂'s tail and before the step, they may well differ);</li><li>the transferred line <b>goes through zero</b>, even if the software's own calibration had an intercept (O₂'s response has one, about −500 ppm: the low levels then come out biased);</li><li>it needs <b>enough injections above the threshold</b> (ten at 0.02 %mol of H₂, 0.012 %mol of O₂): with fewer there is none, and Analysis keeps the table;</li><li>if the <b>software's calibration is wrong, so is this one</b>: it reproduces the table's scale, it does not check it.</li></ul>A Production Rate card takes its values from the nearest Integration card above it; one with none above keeps the tables. ${CONC_HTML}${cardInfo(false, false)}`;
